{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive halftime adjustment",
    "opponent H2-H1 scoring change",
    "team defensive trait reliability",
    "year-over-year correlation of opponent half-to-half scoring differential"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "Can a defense's effect on opponent second-half scoring change be measured reliably as a team trait across seasons?",
    "How much of H2-H1 change variance is attributable to regression to the mean versus defense?",
    "What sample size is needed to detect a stable team-level signal in opponent H2-H1 scoring change?",
    "How to adjust for opponent quality, game script, and home/away to isolate the defensive contribution"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-029",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Team crunch-time PPP delta vs baseline is not a stable organizational identity; YoY r = -0.023 on 62 pairs, deltas within sampling noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-076",
      "status_as_recorded": "rejected",
      "finding": "Observable in-game process/usage/shot-selection/lineup/context changes explain almost no variance beyond RTM in H1->H2 PPP collapse; 65.4% of variance is unexplained, largely idiosyncratic noise."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "Inferring defensive scheme change within game from play-by-play labels to measure adaptation",
      "why": "Label error: inferred labels cannot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently; transition matrices near-uniform consistent with noise."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Half-to-half PPP changes are dominated by regression to the mean and idiosyncratic noise (SF-APPS-076).",
    "Team-specific performance deltas in a particular game segment (crunch time) are not stable year-over-year; the observed correlation is near zero and consistent with sampling noise (SF-APPA-029).",
    "Inferred defensive scheme labels from play-by-play are unreliable for measuring within-game adjustments (SF-APPA-005)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether a defense's effect on opponent H2-H1 scoring change is a reliable trait across seasons; no study has directly examined that specific metric.",
    "That a reliable defensive effect could not exist with a larger sample or a different measurement approach.",
    "The causal attribution of H2-H1 changes to the defense as opposed to offensive fatigue, referee bias, or other factors."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Given the high noise in half-to-half scoring differences and the failure of analogous stability tests, a test of H2-H1 defensive effect reliability will likely be underpowered and inconclusive.",
    "The year-over-year correlation for opponent H2-H1 change can be expected near zero, similar to crunch-time PPP delta, because the game-segment sample per team is limited (~82 games per season) and the signal is small relative to noise.",
    "Adjusting for opponent offensive strength and game context may help but the residual variance is still large, as shown by SF-APPS-076.",
    "Proceeding would risk a null result due to insufficient power or a spurious correlation from uncontrolled confounds."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "True effect size of defensive influence on opponent H2-H1 change may be non-zero but too small to detect with 120 team-seasons.",
    "Potential improvements with advanced player tracking data or larger multi-season samples are unknown.",
    "The SF-APPA-029 result uses crunch-time possessions (100+ per team-season); H2-H1 change would use full halves, but the stability may still be low due to game-to-game variance."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without historical evidence, one might have proceeded to correlate team-season average opponent H2-H1 change across seasons, assuming sufficient power. The retrieval of SF-APPA-029 showing an analogous stability test failed due to noise, and SF-APPS-076 showing that H1->H2 variance is largely unexplained, indicates the trait is likely undetectable, so the decision is not to proceed."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.15,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "120 team-seasons yields ~82 games per team-season, but the effective per-team sample for a reliable H2-H1 change estimate is limited by high game-to-game variance; analogous study with similar sample size found near-zero year-over-year correlation."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent quality, game script (blowouts), home/away, and fatigue may confound the observed H2-H1 change."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Regression to the mean is a major driver of H1->H2 scoring changes, as shown by SF-APPS-076, making it difficult to isolate a true defensive effect."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Half-to-half scoring differential in a single game is a noisy measure of a defense's sustained effect; the reliability of a single game's H2-H1 as a measure of a team trait is low."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF-APPA-029", "FAM:SF-APPS-076"],
    "summary": "Prior work has tested analogous stability of team-specific half-to-half performance deltas (crunch-time PPP delta) and found it untestable due to sampling noise; a variance decomposition of H1->H2 PPP collapse found that regression to the mean and unexplained noise dominate, with negligible contribution from observable defensive process variables. These studies strongly suggest the proposed trait cannot be reliably measured with the available data."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "120 team-seasons yields ~82 games per team-season, but the effective per-team sample for a reliable H2-H1 change estimate is limited by high game-to-game variance; analogous study with similar sample size found near-zero year-over-year correlation."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Opponent quality, game script (blowouts), home/away, and fatigue may confound the observed H2-H1 change."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Regression to the mean is a major driver of H1->H2 scoring changes, as shown by SF-APPS-076, making it difficult to isolate a true defensive effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Half-to-half scoring differential in a single game is a noisy measure of a defense's sustained effect; the reliability of a single game's H2-H1 as a measure of a team trait is low."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.15,
    "untestable": 0.70,
    "artifact": 0.05,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "Year-over-year correlation of a defense's average effect on opponent H2-H1 scoring change, adjusted for opponent offensive rating and home/away status.",
    "unit": "Team-season",
    "sample": "All 120 team-seasons from the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 regular seasons with complete data.",
    "power": "Assuming a true correlation of r=0.3, power is ~80% with n=120 pairs at α=0.05 (one-tailed). However, similar tests (SF-APPA-029) found r ≈ 0, suggesting the true correlation is likely too small to detect with this sample. The design is severely underpowered for realistic effect sizes.",
    "controls": "Adjust each game's opponent H2-H1 margin for opponent season offensive rating and home/away indicator. Use a multi-level model with team random effects and season fixed effects to extract team-specific residuals. Alternatively, compute each team-season's average residualized opponent H2-H1 change and correlate adjacently.",
    "falsification": "Permute team labels within each season to construct a null distribution of year-over-year correlations; observe whether the true correlation exceeds the 95th percentile of the null and is positive.",
    "multiplicity": "Primary test is a single Spearman rank correlation of team residuals across consecutive seasons; no secondary endpoints tested.",
    "decision_rule": "If the Spearman correlation (ρ) between season t and t+1 residualized opponent H2-H1 changes is significantly positive (one-tailed p < 0.05) and ρ ≥ 0.2, declare the trait reliable; otherwise fail to reject the null.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season per-game play-by-play derived data, as available on 2026-10-06."
  }
}}

